# Inner mac_unit assertions are stripped below (chformal -assert -remove
# mac_unit) because that contract is already proven standalone: see
# formal/mac_unit.sby (PASS, auditor-countersigned, EVIDENCE.md mac_unit
# rows). Re-proving 16 replicated copies of the identical mac_unit contract
# inside this tile-level BMC is redundant solver load with no additional
# coverage (CLAUDE.md "formal is a subset / do not fight the tool"): the
# 16 instances are wired identically by the same generate body, so proving
# the contract once, standalone, covers all 16 elaborations of it.
#
# What remains and stays PROVEN here (matmul_tile.sv's own `ifdef FORMAL
# block, tile-level, not touched by the strip below):
#   - reset: all 384 acc_flat bits go to 0
#   - clr && !en: all 384 acc_flat bits go to 0
#   - hold (!en, no clr): acc_flat unchanged
#   - one representative datapath element, (0,0): both the clr&&en load
#     and the clr==0&&en accumulate cases recompute the exact q*k product
#     and re-check it against acc_flat[23:0]
# This covers the shared-control glue over the full 384-bit bus plus one
# spot-check of the broadcast/packing wiring. The remaining 15 datapath
# elements are NOT covered by formal after this change; that is exactly
# what the cocotb TB (tb/matmul_tile/test_matmul_tile.py) exhaustively
# covers instead (all 16 elements, every trial, including the asymmetric
# broadcast-independence test built specifically to catch a swapped/
# transposed (i,j) wiring bug that a single-element formal check cannot).
[options]
mode bmc
depth 5

[engines]
smtbmc z3

[script]
read_verilog -sv -formal -DFORMAL mac_unit.sv matmul_tile.sv
prep -top matmul_tile
chformal -assert -remove mac_unit

[files]
rtl/mac_unit.sv
rtl/matmul_tile.sv
